# Universe content · climate: the water cycle, weather, ocean currents and tides, ice ages, climate zones
# researched: Wikipedia "Water cycle", "Atmospheric circulation" (Hadley, Ferrel and polar cells), "Tropical cyclone",
#   "Ocean current", "Thermohaline circulation", "Upwelling", "Tide", "Milankovitch cycles", "Ice age", "Snowball Earth",
#   "Köppen climate classification" (and Britannica, SKYbrary); "Faint young star paradox" (the faint-young-star problem of our own star)
# Spec: docs/CONTENT-FORMAT.md · wiki/systems/r06-planet.md · docs/TODO.md R6b

# ── The water cycle ──

@water evaporation
name: Evaporation
real: yes
says: the star's heat turns sea and lake water into vapour; warmer air holds more water (about 7% more for each degree)

@water clouds
name: Clouds and rain
real: yes
says: rising air cools, vapour turns to droplets and falls as rain, snow or hail

@water rivers
name: Rivers and lakes
real: yes
says: rain runs downhill into rivers and lakes and back to the sea, carrying worn rock and nutrients

@water ground
name: Ground water
real: yes
says: rain soaks into soil and rock and moves slowly underground, feeding springs and wells

# ── How the air moves ──

@circulation hadley
name: Hadley cell
real: yes
latitude: 0..30
says: hot air rises at the equator (heavy rain, rainforests) and sinks near 30 degrees (dry air, the great deserts)

@circulation ferrel
name: Ferrel cell
real: yes
latitude: 30..60
says: the middle belt; westerly winds and storms, wet again near 60 degrees

@circulation polar
name: Polar cell
real: yes
latitude: 60..90
says: cold air sinks at the poles; little rain or snow, cold deserts

@weather trade-winds
name: Trade winds
real: yes
says: steady winds blowing toward the equator, bent west by the planet's spin

@weather monsoon
name: Monsoon
real: yes
says: winds that reverse with the seasons, bringing a wet season and a dry season

@weather tropical-cyclone
name: Hurricane (tropical cyclone)
real: yes
needs: sea warmer than 26.5 °C, 5 to 20 degrees from the equator
per-year-earth: 80
says: a huge spinning storm with winds over 119 km/h, torrential rain and storm surge

@weather drought
name: Drought
real: yes
says: months or years with too little rain; crops fail and fires spread

@weather blizzard
name: Blizzard
real: yes
says: strong winds and heavy snow in the cold belts

# ── Oceans ──

@ocean surface-currents
name: Surface currents
real: yes
says: winds drive great loops of current (gyres) that carry warm water toward the poles and cold water back (the Gulf Stream warms north-west Europe)

@ocean conveyor
name: The ocean conveyor
real: yes
says: cold, salty water sinks near the poles and creeps along the deep sea floor around the world, taking about a thousand years

@ocean upwelling
name: Upwelling
real: yes
says: where winds push surface water away from a coast or along the equator, cold deep water full of nutrients rises; these are the richest fishing seas

@ocean tides
name: Tides
real: yes
says: the pull of moons and the star raises the sea twice a day; strongest when they line up (spring tides)
earth-open-sea-m: 0.5
earth-largest-m: 16

# ── Ice and long swings ──

@cycle eccentricity
name: Orbit shape
real: yes
period-years: 100000
says: the orbit stretches and rounds again; the main beat of recent ice ages

@cycle obliquity
name: Tilt
real: yes
period-years: 41000
says: the tilt rocks between about 22 and 24.5 degrees; more tilt means stronger seasons

@cycle precession
name: Wobble
real: yes
period-years: 23000
says: the spin axis wobbles like a top, changing which season comes when the planet is nearest its star

@ice glacial
name: Ice age (glacial)
real: yes
says: ice sheets spread from the poles and mountains; on Earth the seas fell about 120 m at the last one
earth-sea-level-drop-m: 120

@ice snowball
name: Snowball world
real: yes
says: ice reaches the equator and the whole world freezes over; volcanoes slowly fill the air with carbon dioxide until it thaws

@star faint-young-star
name: The faint young star
real: yes
says: a young star of one solar mass shines about 30% dimmer; thick greenhouse air kept young worlds from freezing

# ── Climate zones (Köppen) ──

@zone tropical
name: Tropical (A)
real: yes
says: every month 18 °C or warmer; rainforest, monsoon and savanna

@zone arid
name: Arid (B)
real: yes
says: too little rain for the heat; desert and steppe

@zone temperate
name: Temperate (C)
real: yes
says: coldest month between 0 and 18 °C, warmest above 10 °C; mild, with seasons

@zone continental
name: Continental (D)
real: yes
says: coldest month below 0 °C, warmest above 10 °C; hot summers, freezing winters, far from the sea

@zone polar
name: Polar (E)
real: yes
says: warmest month below 10 °C; tundra and ice cap

@zone highland
name: Highland
real: yes
says: high mountains, colder and wetter the higher you go (about 6.5 °C colder per kilometre)

@rule climate-zones
real: rule
says: each place's climate comes from its warmth, its rain and how far it is from the sea; zones shift as continents move, the air changes and ice ages come and go

@rule rain-belts
real: rule
says: rain falls in belts set by how the air turns over: wet at the equator, dry near 30 degrees, wet near 60, dry at the poles

@rule ice-ages
real: rule
says: ice ages follow the orbit's slow swings (100,000, 41,000 and 23,000 years); seas fall as water is locked in ice

@rule currents-heat
real: rule
says: ocean currents carry heat from the warm middle to the poles; coasts are milder than inland; upwelling feeds sea life
